@layout DefaultLayout
@page "/portfolio/havoccrew"

@{
	var canonicalUrl = "https://fydar.dev/portfolio/havoccrew";
	void PageMetadata(IMetadataFactory factory)
	{
		factory.UseTitle("Havoc Crew - Fydar");
		factory.UseDescription("Co-op realtime multiplayer dungeon crawler for the mobile market.");
		factory.UseCanonicalUrl(canonicalUrl);
		factory.UseImage(image =>
		{
			image.Url = "https://fydar.dev/media/havoccrew/social.webp";
			image.Alt = "Promotional material for the game 'Havoc Crew'.";
		});
	}
	var breadcrumbs = new BreadcrumbList()
	{
		new BreadcrumbListItem("Portfolio", "https://fydar.dev/portfolio"),
		new BreadcrumbListItem("Havoc Crew", "https://fydar.dev/portfolio/havoccrew"),
	};
}

<HeadFragment>
	<MetadataComponent Metadata="@PageMetadata" />
	<BreadcrumbListComponent Model="@breadcrumbs" />
</HeadFragment>

<article class="page-systems">

    <div class="fullscreen" data-nosnippet>
        <div class="parallax-frame parallax-focalanchortop" style="--animation-time: 0.5;">
            <picture class="parallax-layer" style="--parallax-multiplier: 0.5">
                <img decoding="async" src="@Assets["media/havoccrew/background.webp"]" />
            </picture>
        </div>

        <div class="fullscreen-footer">
            <div class="container">
                <picture>
                    <img decoding="async" class="titlecard" src="@Assets["media/havoccrew/titlecard.webp"]" alt="Title card for the game 'Havoc Crew'." />
                </picture>

                <div class="titlerow">
                    <div class="flexible-space"></div>
                    <SteamPlayButton href="https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.fortisgames.teamraidrpg&hl=en_GB">Google Play</SteamPlayButton>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="cascade-container">
            <div class="cascade" style="background: linear-gradient(to right, #555d3700 0%, #555d3730 75%, #555d3714 100%), url('media/noise.svg');">
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="container">
        <Block_ArticleHeader>
            <h1 data-nosnippet><cite>Havoc Crew</cite> <span class="subtle">(2026)</span></h1>
        </Block_ArticleHeader>

        <Block_ArticleIntroduction>
            <Sidebar>
                <img decoding="async" class="article-featuredimage" src="@Assets["media/havoccrew/social.webp"]" alt="Promotional material for the game 'Havoc Crew'." />

                <Block_PillCloud>
                    <Pill_Unity />
                    <Pill_CSharp />
                    <Pill_Tools />
                </Block_PillCloud>

                <p>Co-op realtime multiplayer dungeon crawler for the mobile market.</p>

                <menu class="externallinks" aria-label="Project External links">
                    <li><a href="https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.fortisgames.teamraidrpg&hl=en_GB" target="_blank"><SvgSprite Link="/icons.svg?v=1#icon-googleplay" Alt="Google Play" /></a></li>
                </menu>

                <ShareLink Url="@canonicalUrl" />
            </Sidebar>
            <Container>
                <p>At Fortis Games I was the dedicated <b>Tools Programmer</b> on <b>Havoc Crew</b>. I built and maintained tooling for the whole team, from on-device debugging utilities for QA to content authoring tools used daily by designers and engineers.</p>

                <p>The role meant working across every discipline, learning what was slowing each team down and building tools to address it.</p>
            </Container>
        </Block_ArticleIntroduction>

        <Block_ArticleOutlined>
            <Sidebar>
                <aside>
                    <Block_TableOfContents />
                </aside>
            </Sidebar>
            <Content>

                <OutlineSection Id="profile-templates" LinkName="Profile Templates">
                    <Block_ArticleHeading>
                        <hgroup>
                            <p>Developer Testing</p>
                            <h2>Profile Templates</h2>
                        </hgroup>
                    </Block_ArticleHeading>

                    <p>The <b>Profile Templates</b> system captures the complete state of a user's profile as a plain JSON file which can be shared, committed to source control, and re-applied to any developer's account. The team built up a library of reproducible account states for testing specific scenarios.</p>

                    <p>The most useful integration was with the <b>in-game bug reporter</b>. When a QA tester submits a bug report, a profile snapshot is <b>automatically attached</b> alongside the screenshot and logs. A developer can apply that snapshot to their own account and be in the exact state the tester was in. Reproducing account-state-dependent bugs went from a manual, error-prone process to a single click.</p>

                    <Block_Callout>
                        <Icon><SvgSprite Link="/icons.svg?v=1#icon-seal-check-fill" Alt="Check" /></Icon>
                        <ChildContent>Integrated automatic profile capture into the bug reporter, enabling developers to reproduce account-state-dependent bugs instantly from the attached snapshot.</ChildContent>
                    </Block_Callout>

                    <p>Because templates are plain JSON they can also be <b>manually edited</b> to construct precise edge-case states that would be difficult to reach through normal gameplay.</p>
                </OutlineSection>

                <OutlineSection Id="data-editing" LinkName="Data Editing">
                    <Block_ArticleHeading>
                        <hgroup>
                            <p>Content Authoring</p>
                            <h2>Data Editing Tools</h2>
                        </hgroup>
                    </Block_ArticleHeading>

                    <p>I maintained a unified <b>Data Editing Window</b> that was the primary content authoring tool for the game's data-driven systems. I rewrote much of this tool from the ground up, with the ethos that the best way to create a well-integrated and consistent tool was to work with the Unity Engine, rather than working against it.</p>

                    <h3>Tabbed Workspace</h3>

                    <p>The new tool has a <b>persistent tabbed interface</b> with browser-style navigation history. The currently open tabs, their order, and the focused tab all persisted across window closures and Unity restarts, and developers can scrub backwards and forwards through navigation history using the UI controls or mouse thumb buttons.</p>

                    <Block_Callout>
                        <Icon><SvgSprite Link="/icons.svg?v=1#icon-seal-check-fill" Alt="Check" /></Icon>
                        <ChildContent>Introduced persistent tabbed workspaces with navigation history, reducing context-switching overhead when working across complex data hierarchies.</ChildContent>
                    </Block_Callout>

                    <h3>PropertyDrawer Architecture</h3>

                    <p>Working on the unified data editing window surfaced a structural issue common across the codebase. The prevailing pattern for property drawers put everything in one class, including data fetching, writing, input handling, and rendering. Controls built this way could not be reused outside of an inspector context.</p>

                    <p>I split them into two separate concerns. A <b>Control</b> is a standalone <code>VisualElement</code> inheriting from <code>BaseField&lt;T&gt;</code> that owns all visual and interaction logic. A <b>Binder</b> is a minimal <code>PropertyDrawer</code> that just finds the relevant <code>SerializedProperty</code> and calls <code>BindProperty()</code> to wire it up, with no logic of its own.</p>

                    <p>Because the controls are standalone <code>VisualElement</code>s they can be used anywhere in the editor, including custom Editor Windows, Node Graph editors, and inspector drawers.</p>
                    
                    <Block_Callout>
                        <Icon><SvgSprite Link="/icons.svg?v=1#icon-seal-check-fill" Alt="Check" /></Icon>
                        <ChildContent>Established a reusable property drawer architecture separating visual controls from data binding, enabling the same UI controls to be shared across inspector drawers, editor windows, and node graph editors.</ChildContent>
                    </Block_Callout>
                </OutlineSection>

                <OutlineSection Id="rooms-tool" LinkName="Rooms Tool">
                    <Block_ArticleHeading>
                        <hgroup>
                            <p>Content Authoring</p>
                            <h2>Rooms Tool</h2>
                        </hgroup>
                    </Block_ArticleHeading>

                    <p>The <b>Rooms Tool</b> lets designers assemble dungeon levels in the Unity Scene View by snapping pre-fabricated room assets together. Each room exposes a set of <b>junctions</b> that must match between neighbours in rotation and size, so connection errors are impossible by construction.</p>

                    <p>When dragging a room into the scene it <b>automatically snaps</b> to the nearest compatible open junction.</p>

                    <Block_Callout>
                        <Icon><SvgSprite Link="/icons.svg?v=1#icon-seal-check-fill" Alt="Check" /></Icon>
                        <ChildContent>Built a constraint-based Scene View level assembly tool where invalid room connections are impossible by construction.</ChildContent>
                    </Block_Callout>
                </OutlineSection>

                <OutlineSection Id="debug-dialog" LinkName="Debug Dialog">
                    <Block_ArticleHeading>
                        <hgroup>
                            <p>Runtime Debugging</p>
                            <h2>Debug Dialog</h2>
                        </hgroup>
                    </Block_ArticleHeading>

                    <p>The <b>Debug Dialog</b> is a tabbed runtime panel that brings all on-device debug features together in one place, accessible at any point during a play session on mobile and desktop.</p>

                    <p>It is <b>context-aware</b>, meaning that the available options change based on what state the game is in. Different controls appear when the player is inside a dungeon versus in the lobby, and account-specific options are hidden when no user is authenticated. The interface never surfaces controls that can't apply to the current state.</p>

                    <Block_Callout>
                        <Icon><SvgSprite Link="/icons.svg?v=1#icon-seal-check-fill" Alt="Check" /></Icon>
                        <ChildContent>Designed a context-aware runtime debug framework that surfaces only the controls relevant to the player's current game state.</ChildContent>
                    </Block_Callout>

                    <p>The dialog is organised into tabs, where each tab integrated a different subsystem. The <a href="#hero-creator">Hero Creator</a> and <a href="#profile-templates">Profile Templates</a> tools are both accessible through it, making it the single runtime surface for developer and QA workflows.</p>
                </OutlineSection>

                <OutlineSection Id="hero-creator" LinkName="Hero Creator">
                    <Block_ArticleHeading>
                        <hgroup>
                            <p>Developer Testing</p>
                            <h2>Hero Creator</h2>
                        </hgroup>
                    </Block_ArticleHeading>

                    <p>The <b>Hero Creator</b> lets developers and testers instantly generate a fully-configured hero and inject it into their profile, bypassing progression entirely. Testing any specific combination of ascension tier, level, element, and traits requires no grinding through the game to get there.</p>

                    <figure class="figure-image">
                        <picture>
                            <img decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="@Assets["media/havoccrew/heroes.webp"]" alt="The 'A Hero for Every Battle' screenshot from the Google Play page." />
                        </picture>
                        <figcaption>Featured image 3 titled "A HERO FOR EVERY BATTLE" from the <a href="https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.fortisgames.teamraidrpg&hl=en_GB" target="_blank">the Google Play page for Havoc Crew<SvgSprite Link="/icons.svg?v=1#icon-external-link" class="external-link-icon" /></a>.</figcaption>
                    </figure>

                    <Block_Callout>
                        <Icon><SvgSprite Link="/icons.svg?v=1#icon-seal-check-fill" Alt="Check" /></Icon>
                        <ChildContent>Reduced test setup time by enabling instant injection of precisely-configured hero builds without needing to play through progression.</ChildContent>
                    </Block_Callout>
                </OutlineSection>

                <OutlineSection Id="debug-hud" LinkName="Debug HUD">
                    <Block_ArticleHeading>
                        <hgroup>
                            <p>Runtime Debugging</p>
                            <h2>Debug HUD</h2>
                        </hgroup>
                    </Block_ArticleHeading>

                    <p>The <b>Debug HUD</b> was the first tool I built at Fortis Games. It renders numeric overlays onto game entities in both the Unity <b>Scene View</b> and at runtime in editor and non-retail builds using the same uGUI canvas prefab as the Scene View. This tool enables developers and QA can see live game state without adding breakpoints or print statements.</p>

                    <Block_Callout>
                        <Icon><SvgSprite Link="/icons.svg?v=1#icon-seal-check-fill" Alt="Check" /></Icon>
                        <ChildContent>Built a runtime and Scene View overlay system giving developers and QA direct visibility into entity health and AI targeting state.</ChildContent>
                    </Block_Callout>
                </OutlineSection>

            </Content>
        </Block_ArticleOutlined>
    </div>
</article>
